  <p>
    When you visit a website on a desktop computer or connected device without built-in GPS, the server immediately attempts to guess your city and country based on your <strong>IP (Internet Protocol) address</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sometimes this guess is accurate down to your metropolitan area. Other times, it places you 50 to 200 miles away in an adjacent city, or even across international borders. In this guide, we explore the mechanics of GeoIP databases, why IP geolocation is fundamentally non-physical, and how to verify your real IP location using our <a href="/my-location/ip/">IP Location Checker</a>.
  </p>

